  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ACTIONABLE SCIENCE FOR GRASSLAND BIRD CO</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The non-breeding season is pivotal to migratory grassland bird conservation, yet little is known of the limiting factors affecting non-breeding populations and how management actions can best mitigate threats to support stable grassland bird populations across the Chihuahuan Desert. This region is a mosaic of communal and private lands managed for agriculture and ranching. As such, identifying actionable conservation solutions that benefit grassland birds while sustaining local livelihoods is critical. Shrub encroachment represents a major threat to grasslands with economic and environmental consequences. However, uncertainty about the direction and magnitude of bird responses to shrubs across different scales could lead to inefficiencies in shrub management practices, with unintended negative consequences. Partners aim to understand the spatial and temporal scales at which habitat quality (i.e., shrubs) and climate (i.e., precipitation and temperature) drive population and movement dynamics of grassland birds across the Chihuahuan Desert. They will supplement existing grassland bird winter monitoring efforts throughout northern Mexico to determine seasonal shifts in occupancy dynamics in response to weather and management, focusing on Baird&apos;s and Grasshopper Sparrows. Grantees will continue establishing a network of automated radio telemetry (Motus) stations to link local and landscape-level habitat use with demographic and movement data that may underlie regional occupancy dynamics.</narrative>
  </description>
